# Seating Arrangement Reasoning

> Seating arrangement reasoning gives you a set of clues about where people sit — in a row, around a table, or in a grid — and asks you to reconstruct the full arrangement, then answer questions about it.

[important]
Never try to solve a seating arrangement entirely in your head. Sketch it, place the clues one at a time, and update the sketch as each new clue narrows the possibilities. Most errors come from skipping the sketch, not from misunderstanding the clues.
[/important]

## 1. What Seating Arrangement Tests

A typical question gives 5–8 clues about a group of people and one shape (row, circle, square) they're seated in, such as:

```text
8 people sit in a row facing north.
P sits third from the left.
Q sits immediately to the right of P.
R sits at one of the extreme ends.
...
```

The goal is to place every person in exactly one seat, satisfying all clues at once — then answer "who sits next to X," "how many people sit between Y and Z," and similar questions.

## 2. Linear Arrangement Basics

In a straight row, positions are usually numbered left to right, and every person faces one fixed direction (commonly "north," meaning their left/right matches the reader's left/right on the page).

[tip]
Read the facing direction first. If everyone faces **south** instead of north, "left" and "right" flip relative to the page — a clue like "Q is to the left of P" then means Q is to the reader's *right* of P on your sketch.
[/tip]

## 3. Circular / Round-Table Arrangement

In a circular arrangement, there is no fixed "start," so positions are described relative to each other (clockwise/anticlockwise, immediate neighbour, opposite) rather than by a fixed left/right.

@diagram network id="round-table-example" layout="circular" aria="Six people seated around a circular table, labelled with clock-style positions"
node p1 [label="Seat 1"]
node p2 [label="Seat 2"]
node p3 [label="Seat 3"]
node p4 [label="Seat 4"]
node p5 [label="Seat 5"]
node p6 [label="Seat 6"]

edge p1 -> p2
edge p2 -> p3
edge p3 -> p4
edge p4 -> p5
edge p5 -> p6
edge p6 -> p1
@/diagram

[warning]
"Facing the center" and "facing outward" reverse clockwise and anticlockwise relative to each other. A clue like "sitting to the immediate right" means something different for a center-facing group than for an outward-facing one — always confirm which way the group faces before applying a directional clue.
[/warning]

## 4. Facing Center vs Facing Outward

```text
Facing the center:    clockwise motion looks like it goes "left to right" from each person's own view
Facing outward:       clockwise motion looks reversed from each person's own view
```

If the question doesn't say which way the group faces, most exams default to "facing the center" — but always check the question stem for an explicit statement before assuming.

## 5. Clue Types You'll See

| Clue phrase | What it fixes |
|---|---|
| "sits third from the left" | An absolute position (row only) |
| "sits at one of the extreme ends" | One of exactly two possible positions (row only) |
| "immediately to the right/left of" | A relative position, adjacent, one direction only |
| "is a neighbour of" | Adjacent, either direction |
| "sits exactly between X and Y" | An absolute middle position relative to two others |
| "as many people between A and B as between C and D" | A relational count constraint, not a fixed position |
| "sits opposite to" | Circular arrangement only — directly across the table |

## 6. Building an Arrangement Step by Step

1. Draw the shape (row of boxes, or a circle of seats) before reading any clue in detail.
2. Place the clues that fix an **absolute** position first (extreme ends, "third from the left," "exactly between").
3. Then place clues that are **relative** to an already-placed person (immediate neighbour, immediately right of).
4. Save vague or count-based clues ("as many between... as between...") for last — they usually only work once most seats are already filled, since they eliminate options rather than fixing a position directly.
5. If a clue creates two possible arrangements, keep both as branches and use the next clue to eliminate one.

## 7. Double Row Arrangement

Some questions use two parallel rows facing each other (e.g., one row facing north, the other facing south). Clues about "sits opposite to" then link a seat in one row directly to a seat in the other.

```text
Row 1 (facing south): 1  2  3  4
Row 2 (facing north): 5  6  7  8
Seat 1 is opposite Seat 5, Seat 2 opposite Seat 6, and so on.
```

[info]
Because the two rows face each other, "left" and "right" are mirrored between them — what's to Row 1's left is to Row 2's right at the same opposite position.
[/info]

## 8. Solved Example

Six people — A, B, C, D, E, F — sit around a circular table, facing the center.

```text
Clues:
1. A sits third to the right of B.
2. C is an immediate neighbour of both A and D.
3. E sits second to the left of B.
4. F sits between D and B.
```

Working: place B first at any seat (say seat 1, clockwise 1→6). "Third to the right of B" (clockwise) puts A at seat 4. "Second to the left of B" puts E at seat 5 (moving anticlockwise from seat 1 by two). Clue 4 says F is between D and B — since B is at seat 1, and seat 6 or seat 2 are B's immediate neighbours, F must occupy one of those with D on its other side. Clue 2 places C next to both A (seat 4) and D — testing the remaining seats (2, 3, 6) against this constraint pins down C, D, and F uniquely. *(This is exactly the trial-and-branch process from Section 6, step 5 — an absolute clue first, then relative clues to narrow the rest.)*

## 9. Common Mistakes to Avoid

- **Do not** assume "left" and "right" mean the same thing regardless of facing direction — always check which way people face first.
- **Do not** treat "neighbour" as directional — it means adjacent on either side unless the clue specifically says "immediate right" or "immediate left."
- **Do not** finalize an arrangement from only the absolute clues — always check every clue again once the grid is filled, since a later clue may reveal an earlier branch was wrong.
- **Do** keep two (or more) candidate arrangements in mind when a clue is ambiguous, rather than guessing and moving on.

## 10. Quick Reference

| Arrangement shape | Key extra consideration |
|---|---|
| Single row | Fixed left/right, depends on facing direction |
| Circle | No fixed start; use clockwise/anticlockwise and "opposite" |
| Double row | Mirrored left/right between the two rows; "opposite" links rows |

[example]
Working method: **(1)** sketch the shape first, **(2)** place absolute-position clues, **(3)** place relative-position clues anchored to those, **(4)** use count/comparison clues last to break remaining ties, **(5)** re-check every clue against the finished sketch.
[/example]
